{{also|câble|câblé|cablé|Cable}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*keh₂p-}}
Recorded since c.1205 as {{inh|en|enm|cable}}, from {{der|en|fro-nor|cable}}, from {{der|en|LL.|capulum||lasso, rope, halter}}, from {{der|en|la|capiō||to take, seize}}.  Use of the term "cable" to refer to the USD/GBP exchange rate originated in the mid-19th century, when the exchange rate began to be transmitted across the Atlantic by a submarine communications cable.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkeɪ.bəl/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-cable.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪbəl|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|material}} A long object used to make a physical connection.
## A strong, large-diameter [[wire]] or [[rope]], or something resembling such a rope.
##: {{syn|en|wire rope|cord|Thesaurus:string}}
## An [[assembly]] of two or more [[cable-laid]] ropes.
## An assembly of two or more wires, used for electrical power or data circuits; one or more and/or the whole may be insulated.
## {{lb|en|nautical}} A strong rope or [[chain]] used to [[moor]] or [[anchor]] a [[ship]].
##: {{cot|en|hawser}}
##* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=9|passage=“And now the time of tide has come; the ship casts off her '''cables'''; and from the deserted wharf the uncheered ship for Tarshish, all careening, glides to sea.}}
# {{lb|en|communications}} A system for transmitting [[television]] or [[Internet]] services over a network of [[coaxial]] or [[fibreoptic]] cables.
#: {{ux|en|I tried to watch the movie last night but my '''cable''' was out.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2014-03-15|volume=410|issue=8878|magazine=w:The Economist|title=Turn it off|url=http://www.economist.com/news/leaders/21598997-american-regulators-should-block-comcasts-proposed-deal-time-warner-cable-turn-it|passage=If the takeover is approved, Comcast would control 20 of the top 25 '''cable''' markets, {{...}}. Antitrust officials will need to consider Comcast’s status as a monopsony (a buyer with disproportionate power), when it comes to negotiations with programmers, whose channels it pays to carry.}}
## {{lb|en|TV}} {{ellipsis of|en|cable television|nodot=1}}, broadcast over the above network, not by antenna.
# {{senseid|en|telegram}} A [[telegram]], notably when sent by (submarine) telegraph cable.
#: {{syn|en|cablegram}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A unit of [[length]] equal to one tenth of a [[nautical mile]].
#: {{syn|en|cable length}}
# {{lb|en|unit|chiefly|nautical}} 100 [[fathom]]s, 600 imperial feet, approximately 185 m.
# {{lb|en|finance}} The [[currency]] pair British Pound against United States Dollar.
# {{lb|en|architecture}} A [[moulding]], [[shaft]] of a [[column]], or any other member of [[convex]], [[rounded]] [[section]], made to resemble the spiral twist of a rope.
# {{lb|en|knitting}} A textural pattern achieved by passing groups of stitches over one another.

